The Ember is the first arrival from an entire world in flight. Its failing light is a warning of something much larger approaching—and its inhabitants may be as frightened of that arrival as Isaac eventually becomes.

I’d build the story around this central connection:

> Isaac is trying to restore a light that keeps people alive. Above him, another keeper is attempting the same task on an unimaginable scale.

The Ember and the departing world

Their civilisation knew its home star was dying. They had already surrounded it with a vast energy-harvesting structure: a Dyson sphere built over generations, supporting their planet and an enormous surrounding population.

When saving the star became impossible, they began an evacuation unlike anything humanity could imagine.

Many vessels departed first: scouts, seed ships, industrial ships and crowded refugee arks. They scattered towards possible destinations. Some were searching for resources; others for habitable worlds. Their journeys were uncertain enough that no single expedition could carry the civilisation’s future.

The Ember was one of the vessels sent to find a living destination.

Behind those early departures came the greater undertaking: their home planet itself, accompanied by its population and the mobile structure that had once enclosed their star. Its immense articulated sections now travel together as a world-bearing vessel.

That last element is your deliberate leap into advanced science fiction: moving a planet and a star-enclosing structure requires extraordinary propulsion and engineering. You can establish that capability without pretending it follows from present-day fusion research.

The inhabitants call this larger vessel the Hearth—a working name that pairs naturally with The Ember. To them it means home. To humanity, it could mean the end of daylight.

What happened to The Ember

The Ember discovers the Sun and then Earth: a stable energy source, a living world and an inhabited civilisation.

Its mission should have ended with a survey and a report. Instead, a major failure leaves it stranded near Earth for an undefined length of time.

I’d make that failure a breakdown in the machinery that distributes power and removes heat. The artificial sun still burns, but the vessel can no longer safely deliver its full output.

That creates several connected problems:

• Habitats are progressively shut down to preserve the remaining population.
• Propulsion cannot operate at the power needed to leave.
• The long-range transmitter cannot safely send its full distress signal.
• Repair equipment competes with life support for the same limited capacity.

The Ember can survive, but survival steadily consumes its options.

Its keeper must choose which places remain warm, which places remain lit, and which people must move into increasingly crowded surviving districts.

They have not lost their sun. They are losing the ability to live around it.

The message that changes everything

The Ember has two possible routes to survival.

It can repair itself using resources and manufacturing obtained near Earth. That takes time, contact and cooperation—possibly with people who have good reasons to fear it.

Or it can restore enough transmission capacity to call the Hearth.

The second option is faster once the signal succeeds, but the message would contain more than a plea for rescue. It would confirm:

We have found a suitable star. There is a living world here. Come to us.

The Hearth is already travelling towards this region, following an earlier survey route or preliminary report. The Ember’s complete transmission would give it the confirmation and precise destination needed to commit to the Sun.

That distinction gives the story room to breathe: the larger vessel is coming this way, but its arrival at our Sun is not yet inevitable.

Inside The Ember, people disagree about sending the message. Some believe their duty is to save their own population at any cost. Others understand what “successful arrival” would mean for Earth.

What “consuming the Sun” means

I’d keep that phrase in the story, particularly in frightened human accounts. The eventual revelation is more unsettling than a creature swallowing a star.

The Hearth would open its travelling structure and deploy around the Sun, rebuilding its energy-harvesting enclosure. Their planet would become part of a restored home system.

It would not need to extinguish the Sun to endanger Earth. Intercepting the sunlight Earth depends upon would be enough. Whether Earth receives a protected opening, controlled illumination or no provision at all becomes a political decision made by the arriving civilisation.

Their salvation would give them control over humanity’s daylight.

This also prevents the Hearth from being automatically evil. Its people have endured an extraordinary evacuation. They may believe they are finally reaching safety. Some may not know Earth exists; others may have been assured that its inhabitants can be accommodated.

How Isaac’s opening carries the larger story

At first, Isaac’s task remains immediate and human: restore the lighthouse before people die at sea.

The Ember crosses the Moon above him. It appears silent and complete, although whole districts inside it are dark.

As the story develops, Isaac discovers that the vessel also has a keeper—and that this keeper is trying to restore a light, preserve a population and decide whether to send a signal.

I would avoid making the lighthouse burner directly compatible with alien machinery. The connection is stronger as a shared responsibility, with communication or contact developing separately.

Eventually Isaac understands that helping the other keeper could save thousands aboard The Ember while summoning something that threatens everyone below.

The question at the heart of the game becomes:

When keeping your own people alive endangers another world, what does a keeper owe to the people beyond their light?